            synth_mode - IF synthesizer mode (DBBC3 racks)

 Syntax:     synth_mode=loX,freq,output,check
             synth_mode=
             synth_mode=loX
 Response:   synth_mode/loX,freq,output,ext_lo,pre-filer_sb,att,mode,ref_source,ref_freq,ref_doubler,ref_divider,offset,lock

Settable Parameters:

  loX       Synthesizer for the LO (IF) to set/monitor/check: 'loa', 'lob',
            ..., up to the last IF in the system ('loh' is the last possible),
            or "next' to read the synthesizer after the previous one
            monitored/checked (wrapping after the last). 'next' cannot be used
            with 'force' (for the 'check' parameter) at all. 'next' cannot be
            used with 'check' unless the 'freq' and 'output' are '*'s
            (previous values). No default. No previous value ('*') available
            for this parameter.

  freq      Frequency in MHZ for the synthesizer. No default unless 'ifX=...'
            has been setup and/or 'output' is 'disable'. If 'ifX' input '2' is
            selected and 'loX' has been defined with 'lo=loX,...', the
            frequency set for 'lo=loX,...' is the default unless an
            'external-LO' has been defined (see 'help=extlo'). If one has been
            defined, the default frequency is adjusted accordingly.  If 'ifX'
            input '1' or 'output' is 'disable', the default is "undefined."
            For Monitoring and Checking (see below in Comments), the value is
            shown in parentheses if 'output' is 'disable'.

  output    'disable' or 'enable'. No default unless 'ifX=...' is setup. If
            'ifX' input '2' is selected, the default is 'enable'; input '1',
            'disable'.

  check     Either 'check', 'force' or 'force_plus'. Default is 'check'.
            'force' and 'force_plus' cannot be used with 'next' (for the 'loX'
            parameter). 'check' cannot be used with 'next' unless the 'freq'
            and 'output' are '*'s (previous values). Using 'force' always sets
            the output enable. If 'output' is 'enable', it also sets the
            frequency.

            It is recommended to NOT use 'force_plus'. If it is used, it
            behaves the same as 'force' except that if 'output' is 'enable',
            it also sets the device to have the nominal values for 'mode',
            'ref_source', 'ref_freq', 'ref_doubler' 'ref_divider', and
            'freq_offset'.

            If parameter value is 'check', the state of the device output
            enable is always checked against the command parameter.

            If parameter value is 'check' and if 'output' _should_ be 'enable'
            and _is_: the following parameters from the device are also
            checked:
                (i) 'freq'
               (ii) 'mode' is 'CW",
              (iii) 'ref_source' is 'external'
               (iv) 'ref_freq' is '10'
                (v) 'ref_doubler' is 'enable'
               (vi) 'ref_divider' is 'disable'
              (vii) 'offset' is '0'
             (viii) 'lock' status is 'locked'.

Monitor-only Parameters:

  ext_lo        The frequency in MHz and the side-band ('usb' or 'lsb') of the
                corresponding external-LO (from the 'ext_lo' command), if one
                applies. If so, the information is shown in parenthesis
                because it is not read from the device with a slash between
                the frequency and side-band, for example, '(38100/usb)'. The
                external-LO is determined when the synthesizer state is
                defined with a default frequency when the corresponding 'ifX'
                input is '2'. If no external-LO applies, the parameter is
                null.

  pre-filter_sb The inferred pre-filter side-band ('usb' or 'lsb'), relative,
                to 'freq' that should be used with GCoMo 'RF-in' for the
                corresponding external-LO (from the 'ext_lo' command) ,if one
                applies. If so, the value is shown in parenthesis because it
                is not read from the device; it is calculated when the
                synthesizer state is defined with a default frequency and the
                corresponding 'ifX' input is '2'. The pre-filter must be
                supplied externally.

                If no external-LO applies, the parameter is null and the
                direct receiver output should be connected to the GCoMo
                'IF-in'.

                See "Pre-filter Side-band" in Comments for more details.

  att           attenuation, '0.0' through '31.5' dB in '0.5' dB steps.

  mode          'CW', 'SWEEP', or 'LIST' (or 'unknown', if not one of three
                preceding values). For 'check', any value except 'CW' will
                generate an error.

  ref_source    The reference signal source: 'internal' or 'external'.  For
                'check', any value except 'external' will generate an error .

  ref_freq      The reference signal frequency in MHz. For 'check', any value
                except '10' will generate an error.

  ref_doubler   Reference doubler: 'disable' or 'enable'. For 'check', any
                value except 'enable' will generate an error.

  ref_divider   Reference divider: 'disable' or 'enable'. For 'check', any
                value except 'disable' will generate an error.

  offset        Frequency offset in MHz. For 'check', any value except '0'
                will generate an error.

  lock          Synthesizer status: 'locked' or 'unlocked' (or 'unknown', if
                not one of the two preceding values).  For 'check', an error
                 will be generated if the value is not 'locked'.

Comments:

CAUTION: It is recommended to NOT use 'force_plus'.

WARNING: This command should not be used when recording data since calibration
data may be lost.

In addition to the normal 'setting' and 'monitoring' forms of most commands,
this one has an additional 'checking' form. The details of these forms are
summarized below.

  Setting -- The setting form is invoked by using a literal 'force' for the
             'check' parameter and will command the device. The values of
             'freq' and 'output' also set the FS model (and hence the values
             for '*' in subsequent commands) for that IF. Using '*' if the
             synthesizer values for that IF has not previously been defined is
             an error for setting. Using 'next' is not permitted.

             If 'output' is 'disable', no 'freq' value is sent to the device.

             The corresponding LO value ('lo=loX,...'), has not been set, and
             the 'freq' and 'output' parameters are default (null), the
             command is a no-op.  See comments below about using a specific
             IF.

             Similarly, if the synthesizer parameters have not been set
             using '*' for any parameter makes the command a no-op. See
             comments below about using a specific IF.

  Checking -- The 'checking' form, which is the default, is invoked by using a
              literal 'check' (or null value, i.e., the default) for the
              'check' parameter.

              The state of the device output enable is always checked against
              the command parameter.

              If 'output' _should_ be 'enable' and _is_: the following values
              are also checked:
                  (i) 'freq'
                 (ii) 'mode' is 'CW",
                (iii) 'ref_source' is 'external'
                 (iv) 'ref_freq' is '10'
                  (v) 'ref_doubler' is 'enable'
                 (vi) 'ref_divider' is 'disable'
                (vii) 'offset' is '0'
               (viii) 'lock' status is 'locked'.

              Any deviations from what is expected are reported as errors.

              Just as for Setting, the values of 'freq' and 'output' set the
              FS model for that IF.

              'next' can be specified for the IF for checking, with '*' for
              'freq' and 'output'. See below about the 'checkXXX' procedure.

              If a specific IF is used (not 'next') and the corresponding LO
              value ('lo=loX,...'), has not been set, and the 'freq' and
              'output' parameters are default (null), the command is a no-op.
              See comments below about using a specific IF.

              Similarly, if a specific IF is used (not 'next') and the
              synthesizer parameters have not been set, using '*' for any
              parameter makes the command a no-op. See comments below about
              using a specific IF.

  Monitoring -- The monitor forms are 'synth_mode' (for all IFs) or
                'synth_mode=loX' (with no following parameters). This form
                does not set the FS model or report errors in the device
                configuration. Using 'next' for 'loX' is permitted.

Using 'synth_mode=' clears the FS model for all synthesizers. It should be
used before setting the synthesizers for a mode so that unused synthesizers
don't have their definitions carried over from a previous mode.

The IF for 'next' is the next one after the last IF monitored or checked. In
other words, it is shared between monitor and checking. It advances even if
there is a communication error so that it doesn't become stuck on one IF.

For a specific IF, i.e., not 'next', if the 'freq' and 'output' parameters are
null (default) and the LO frequency has not been defined for this IF
('lo=...'), the command is a no-op. This allows which synthesizer are used to
be controlled by which IFs are in use without modifying these commands in the
setup procedure. The synthesizer setup should start with a 'synth_mode='
command, followed by commands similar to:

  synth_mode=loa,,,check

for all IFs in the system, whether being used or not. See details the
'dbbc3_synth' procedure below.

Also for a specific IF, i.e., not 'next', if synthesizer values have not been
set and '*' is used for any of them, the command is a no-op. This allows a
fixed procedure to check only the synthesizers in use. Commands similar to

  synth_mode=loa,*,*,check

are used for all IFs in the system. There will only be output for synthesizers
with defined setups. If no synthesizer have been defined, there will be no
output. You can add a command at the end of the procedure to get some feedback
when it is done:

  op_stream="Synthesizer checking complete. Only defined synthesizers were checked."

An example 'synth_check' procedure for eight IFs is included in the example
procedure libraries 'd3fbstation.prc' and 'dbbc3st.prc' in
'/usr2/fs/st.default/proc'.

Similarly, all the synthesizers with defined setups can be set with commands
like:

  synth_mode=loa,*,*,force

An example 'synth_force' procedure for eight IFs is included in the example
procedure libraries 'd3fbstation.prc' and 'dbbc3st.prc' in
'/usr2/fs/st.default/proc'.

If 'next' is used with 'check', IFs that have not been setup will be skipped.
This allows the use of:

  synth_mode=next,*,*,check

to cycle through only the setup IFs. See more about the 'checkXXX' procedure
below.

The frequency sent to, and read from, the device is half the value set/shown
in this command, 'freq'. The latter is the effective frequency of the
synthesizer.

The 'att' value is set in the boot configuration files and should have been
determined in the laboratory before shipping.

                             Pre-filter Side-band

The signal from the receiver, RF or an IF generated from an LO external to the
DBBC3, can either be directly fed into the GCoMo 'IF-in' connector or through
the DBBC3 double-side-band mixer for further down-conversion. If directly fed
and RF, the signal is USB. If directly fed from an external LO, the incoming
signal is either USB or LSB. For these direct fed cases, the 'ifX=1,...'
command is used. If it is fed through the mixer, is connected through an
external USB or LSB pre-filter, relative to the DBBC3 synthesizer frequency,
to the 'RF-in' connector; the 'RF-out' connector is jumpered to the 'IF-in'
connector. For this case, the 'ifX=2,...' command is used.

The FS 'lo=...' command describes the net-LO frequency, and net-side-band,
before the BBCs. This includes the effect of any external-LO and the DBBC3
synthesizer, if used. The FS 'ext_lo' command describes the external-LO
frequency, and output side-band, of the mixing steps before the DBBC3. Note
that the external-LO may be the result of one or more mixing steps. The only
thing that matters is their cumulative effect, which is what the 'ext_lo=...'
command describes. The FS 'synth_mode=..' command describes the DBBC3
synthesizer frequency and external pre-filter side-band required. The
pre-filter must be supplied by the user.

The following table identifies the side-band (SB) of the external pre-filter
required before the DBBC3 synthesizer depending on the relationship of the
'ext_lo' (external-LO) and the 'lo' (net-LO) frequencies and their output
side-bands.  Only some cases are possible. Even for cases that are possible,
the bandpasses of the external-LO output and the pre-filter must be compatible
to get a useful result. Cases that are not possible are included in the table
for completeness with 'NO' and an explanation in the Pre-filter SB column.
Please note the restriction on the last row.

    Frequencies  ext_lo SB  LO SB        Pre-filter SB
                                   (relative to |ext_lo-lo|)
    ext_lo < lo     USB      USB              USB
    ext_lo < lo     USB      LSB              LSB
    ext_lo < lo     LSB      USB     NO: no side-band overlap
    ext_lo < lo     LSB      LSB   NO: up-conversion unavailable

    ext_lo = lo     USB      USB       synthesizer not used
    ext_lo = lo     USB      LSB     NO: no side-band overlap
    ext_lo = lo     LSB      USB     NO: no side-band overlap
    ext_lo = lo     LSB      LSB       synthesizer not used

    ext_lo > lo     USB      USB   NO: up-conversion unavailable
    ext_lo > lo     USB      LSB     NO: no side-band overlap
    ext_lo > lo     LSB      USB              LSB
    ext_lo > lo     LSB      LSB      USB (only for lo>0)

    If the synthesizer is used, its frequency is always positive, that is,
    |lo - ext_lo|, a pre-filter is used before 'RF-in', 'RF-out' is jumpered
    to 'IF-in' and FS command 'ifX=2,...' is used. If the synthesizer is not
    used, that is, 'ext_lo and 'lo' are equal, or there is no external-LO, the
    signal is connected directly to 'IF-in' and FS command 'if=1,...' is used.

                         'dbbc3_synth' SNAP procedure

  The 'dbbc3_synth' SNAP procedure in the 'station' procedure library is
  intended to be used in to check that the configuration of the synthesizers
  agrees with the settings of the 'lo=loX,...' and 'ifX=...' commands for each
  IF. It is called by 'drudg' generated setup procedure for DBBC3 racks (from
  the 'ifdXX' procedure). The procedure should contain:

    synth_mode=
    synth_mode=loa,,,check
    ...

  With the 'loa' command repeated for each additional IF up to the number of
  IFs that your system has (as set in the 'dbbc3.ctl' control file). For IFs
  that don't have a first LO defined (with 'lo=...'), the command for that
  synthesizer will be a no-op.

  Any 'ext_lo=...' commands to define external-LOs that may used should
  precede the 'synth_mode=' command above. See 'help=ext_lo' for information
  on the 'ext_lo' command.

  You should verify that the 'drudg' generated 'ifdXX' procedure works with
  the 'synth_mode=...' (and any needed 'ext_lo=...') commands in 'dbbc3_synth'
  before observing.

  An example 'dbbc3_synth' procedure for eight IFs (and no external LOs) is
  included in the example procedure libraries 'd3fbstation.prc' and
  'dbbc3st.prc' in '/usr2/fs/st.default/proc'.

  If appropriate, you can use non-default values for 'freq' and 'output' in
  'dbbc3_synth'.

  If need be, you can use 'force' to command instead of just checking in
  'dbbc3_synth'. In that case, you should follow that group of commands with
  'check' commands for all the IFs in your system. For example for 'loa' you
  would use:

     synth_mode=loa,*,*,check

                          'checkXXX' SNAP procedure

  You can implement cyclical checking of the synthesizers in the 'checkXXX'
  ('XXX' corresponds to your recorder type) in the 'station' procedure
  library. This is needed because the 'synth_mode' command: (i) interferes
  with multicast transmission (so it should not be used during recording), and
  (2) takes up to about two seconds per IF (so at most one IF should be
  checked each time 'checkXXX' is used).

  The command that should be used is:

    synth_mode=next,*,*,check

  Only IFs with defined synthesizer setups will be checked. Using asterisks,
  '*', is required and allows it to work with previously set (or checked)
  values of 'freq' and 'output', whether defaults or not.

  If you use this command in 'checkxxx', you should verify first that the time
  required doesn't delay schedule execution.
